    "media:imagetext": "SFO\nSAN\nFRANCISCO\nINTERNATIONAL\nAIRPORT\nINNER ROADS\nThe sketch at left shows the elevated inner road, a new 3-lane\nstructure to provide express lanes for the departure level of\nthe airport. This will allow more curb space for drop-off on\nthe terminal road and ease congestion - allowing \"thru\"\ntraffic to move on the inner road loop, either to destinations\nfurther downstream or to leave the Complex.\nRAPID TRANSIT\nThe continuing rapid growth of the San Francisco International\nAirport shows that ground access improvements are a major\nneed. It is clear that rapid transit service must be provided if\nthe airport is to reach its optimum regional use as an air-ground\ntransportation transfer point without falling to vehicular\naccess restrictions.\nAn extension of BART from Daly City to a stub-end station at\nthe upper level of the Terminal and Transportation Center is\nfunctionally feasible but not yet approved or financed.\nThe top photo shows a possible airport interchange at Bayshore\nFreeway which weaves auto and rapid transit structures into a\nsweeping entry design that leads into the Terminal complex.\nThe second picture is a mezzanine level plan of the future\nTerminal and Transportation Center which could accommodate\nBART. A vehicular people mover system is shown on the main\nconcourse below.\n30/0-00\nMEZZANINE LEVEL PLAN",
